<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Days</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Today Is Friday!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of simple sentences. Reading: Follow written instructions with visual aids. Phonemic Awareness: Recognize all the letters of the alphabet in isolation and the correspondence between letter and sound at the beginning of words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show comprehension of the days of the week in simple texts by matching sentences with visual aids (e.g., matching "Yesterday was Wednesday." to a visual that highlights the previous day). Can identify and respond to key words about days in simple texts using visual support (e.g., pointing to "Tomorrow is Friday." in a schedule). Can recognize all the letters of the alphabet and connect each letter to its initial sound in familiar words (e.g., identifying the letter F and its /f/ sound in "Friday" when shown a weekly calendar).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.5em; margin-top: 25px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students cheerfully, "Good morning, everyone! How are you today?" The teacher points to a large classroom calendar and says, "Look at our calendar! What day is it today?" The teacher sings a short 'Days of the Week' song with actions (e.g., clapping for Monday, jumping for Tuesday), encouraging students to participate in TPR. The teacher then focuses on the current day, "Today is Friday!" and shows a flashcard of "Friday."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards with sentences like "Today is Friday." and "Yesterday was Thursday." The teacher reads each sentence clearly, pointing to each word and emphasizing the pronunciation of /f/ in "Friday" and /w/ in "Wednesday" (if applicable, using a previous day's flashcard). The teacher then models how to match a sentence like "Today is Friday." to a picture of children playing and smiling, indicating happiness on Friday.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals and simple questions.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of "Friday"): "Is today Monday?" (Expected: "No!") "What day is today?" (Expected: "Friday!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a sentence "Yesterday was Wednesday." and a picture of Wednesday): "Was yesterday Thursday?" (Expected: "No!") "What day was yesterday?" (Expected: "Wednesday!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: School Days)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, simple visual "School Schedule for the Week" poster with days of the week, simple sentences, and corresponding pictures (e.g., "Monday: Math class" with a picture of numbers; "Tuesday: Science class" with a picture of a beaker; "Friday: Art class" with a picture of paints). The text also includes sentences like "Today is Friday. I like Friday! Art is on Friday!" and "Yesterday was Thursday. We had English class."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Talking about schedules: Communicating about daily classroom activities and schedules using appropriate language and turn-taking (e.g., "Today is Monday.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher points to the schedule and asks, "What is this about? Is it about animals or school?" (Expected: "School!"). The teacher then points to different days, "What day is this?" (Expected: "Monday!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False with Visuals:</b> The teacher reads a sentence from the schedule, e.g., "Today is Friday." and students show a "True" or "False" card, or thumbs up/down, based on the schedule shown. "Friday: Art class." (Picture shows art). Teacher asks, "Is Math on Friday?" (Students respond "False" or "No").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Pointing):</b> The teacher reads a sentence, "Yesterday was Thursday." Students point to "Thursday" on the visual schedule. The teacher reads, "English class." Students point to the day with English class.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Letter-Sound Connection:</b> The teacher points to "Friday" on the schedule and asks, "What letter is this?" (pointing to 'F'). "What sound does F make?" (Expected: /f/). The teacher repeats for 'Wednesday' and /w/.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of simple sentences about days of the week and school activities. Grammar: Past simple "was", Present simple "is". Vocabulary: Monday-Sunday, calendar, schedule, activity, school, math, science, English, like. Pronunciation: /w/ in "Wednesday", /f/ in "Friday". Phonemic awareness: correspondence between letter and sound at the beginning of words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Read and Match":</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with two columns. Column A has simple sentences (e.g., "Today is Monday.", "Yesterday was Wednesday.", "Tomorrow is Friday. I like Friday!"). Column B has corresponding small pictures (e.g., a calendar highlighting Monday, kids happy, a specific activity for Wednesday). Students draw a line to match each sentence with its correct picture. The teacher circulates, providing individual help.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – "Sentence Completion with Pictures":</b> The teacher projects or distributes a worksheet with simple sentences that have a blank, accompanied by three picture choices (e.g., "Today is ____." with pictures of Monday, Friday, and Sunday). Students read the sentence and circle or write the correct day/activity from the picture choices. Example: "We learn _______ on Tuesday." (pictures of math, science, art). This activity reinforces reading key vocabulary within context. The teacher also includes sentences like "This word starts with /f/. It is ______." (pictures of 'Friday' and 'Wednesday').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check – "Read Aloud & Verify":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of 5-6 flashcards, each with a simple sentence (e.g., "Today is Wednesday.", "English class is fun!", "I like school."). One student reads a sentence aloud to their partner. The partner then points to a large classroom calendar or a picture illustrating that day/activity. They check each other's reading and comprehension. The teacher encourages clear pronunciation, especially for words like "Friday" and "Wednesday."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: School Days)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Favorite Day Reading & Drawing Activity." Students will read simple sentences about their favorite school day and draw a corresponding picture. This connects directly to "Today Is Friday!" and the "School Days" scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a worksheet titled "My Favorite School Day." The worksheet has two simple sentences to complete and a large box for drawing.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sentence 1: "My favorite day is __________." (Students fill in a day, e.g., "Friday").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sentence 2: "On __________ I like to _________." (Students fill in the day again and an activity, e.g., "On Friday I like to draw." or "On Friday I like to play with my friend.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below the sentences, students draw a picture illustrating their favorite day and activity. For example, if they wrote "My favorite day is Friday. On Friday I like to draw," they would draw themselves drawing on Friday.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once completed, students can share their worksheet with a partner, reading their sentences aloud and showing their drawing. The partner verifies if the sentences match the drawing.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Favorite Day Reading & Drawing Activity" and a quick "Read and Point" exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students submit their "My Favorite School Day" worksheet. The teacher assesses their ability to read and complete simple sentences accurately and their comprehension demonstrated by the drawing.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Exit Ticket:</b> Students receive a small card with 3-4 simple sentences about days and activities (e.g., "Today is Friday. I like to play.", "Yesterday was Wednesday. We had science."). They also have a small sheet with corresponding visuals (e.g., a happy child, a science book). Students read each sentence and point to or circle the correct visual aid. Additionally, they identify words starting with the /f/ or /w/ sound.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Student correctly matches sentences with visual aids.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Student uses target vocabulary (days of the week, school activities) correctly in their sentences.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Student can identify words starting with the /f/ sound (e.g., "Friday") and /w/ sound (e.g., "Wednesday").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Following Instructions:</b> Student follows written instructions with visual aids (completes sentences, draws matching picture).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I read about the days of the week?" (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I find the letter 'F' in words like 'Friday'?" (Circle: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you read today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is your favorite day to read about?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging students in reading comprehension? (e.g., specific visuals, matching activities).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face in showing comprehension of simple sentences or following written instructions? How can I adjust scaffolding for students who struggled? (e.g., more 1-on-1 reading, larger fonts, simpler sentence structures).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effective were the phonemic awareness activities in connecting letters to initial sounds?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This lesson focused on reading comprehension of days of the week and school activities. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students actively use the vocabulary and grammar (days of the week, "Today is...", "Yesterday was...", "I like...") in spoken interactions and simple dialogues about their school schedules and preferences, making connections between the written word and spoken communication.</p>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>